en"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"formula","orig":"e ( G ) ≤ 3 n -6 ≤ 3 n -6 -n 36 k 1+log 2 3 + k 3 4 ,","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":369.50934,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":210.71976999999998,"r":401.27942128,"t":394.3905475},"charspan":[0,53],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/64","text":""},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"the result holds. So, we assume that","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":353.06948,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":70.91976999999997,"r":229.70420418799634,"t":363.03211999999996},"charspan":[0,36],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/65","text":"the result holds. So, we assume that"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"□","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":466.08013000000005,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":533.40006,"r":541.1410312800001,"t":472.92446368000003},"charspan":[0,1],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/66","text":"□"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"formula","orig":"n > 36 k log 2 3 . (2)","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":329.90928,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":277.19976999999994,"r":541.1051129111503,"t":341.71040750000003},"charspan":[0,22],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/67","text":""},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"Let G be a 2 C k -free plane graph with e ( G ) = ex P ( n, 2 C k ). It is clear that G is connected; otherwise we can add an edge between two components of G to make it remain 2 C k -free, a contradiction. If G is C k -free, then by Theorem 1.3, e ( G ) ≤ 3 n -6 -n 4 k log 2 3 ≤ 3 n -6 -n 36 k 1+log 2 3 + k 2 2 , the result holds. So, we assume that G contains a C k .","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":263.1771845999999,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":70.91931,"r":541.208416905724,"t":316.59182},"charspan":[0,371],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/68","text":"Let G be a 2 C k -free plane graph with e ( G ) = ex P ( n, 2 C k ). It is clear that G is connected; otherwise we can add an edge between two components of G to make it remain 2 C k -free, a contradiction. If G is C k -free, then by Theorem 1.3, e ( G ) ≤ 3 n -6 -n 4 k log 2 3 ≤ 3 n -6 -n 36 k 1+log 2 3 + k 2 2 , the result holds. So, we assume that G contains a C k ."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"section_header","level":1,"orig":"Case 1. G is 2 -connected.","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":242.18921999999998,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":70.91933,"r":187.48804299278407,"t":252.15185999999994},"charspan":[0,26],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/69","text":"Case 1. G is 2 -connected."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"In that case, the boundary of each face in G is a cycle. Assume that c is the smallest number of vertices that need to be removed from G to make it C k -free, and let v 1 , v 2 , . . . , v c be one such set of vertices. Then c ≤ k , since we can remove vertices of a k -cycle to make it C k -free. Moreover, for any i, j ∈ [ c ], there exists a k -cycle in G such that v i is contained in the k -cycle, but v j not.","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":177.25717459999998,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":70.91883000000001,"r":541.2842161464317,"t":230.67175999999995},"charspan":[0,415],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/70","text":"In that case, the boundary of each face in G is a cycle. Assume that c is the smallest number of vertices that need to be removed from G to make it C k -free, and let v 1 , v 2 , . . . , v c be one such set of vertices. Then c ≤ k , since we can remove vertices of a k -cycle to make it C k -free. Moreover, for any i, j ∈ [ c ], there exists a k -cycle in G such that v i is contained in the k -cycle, but v j not."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"section_header","level":1,"orig":"Case 2.1. c = 1.","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":152.18921999999998,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":70.91911000000005,"r":145.91879098821528,"t":162.15185999999994},"charspan":[0,16],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/71","text":"Case 2.1. c = 1."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"Since G is 2-connected, it follows that G ′ = G -v 1 is connected and is C k -free. Assume that G ′ has r blocks B 1 , B 2 , . . . , B r , where | B i | ≥ k log 2 3 for i ∈ [ q ] and | B i | < k log 2 3 for q +1 ≤ i ≤ r . For each i ∈ [ q ], since B i is a C k -free 2-connected plane graph, it follows that","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":97.69708460000004,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":70.91888000000017,"r":541.0781825600001,"t":138.07046749999995},"charspan":[0,307],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/72","text":"Since G is 2-connected, it follows that G ′ = G -v 1 is connected and is C k -free. Assume that G ′ has r blocks B 1 , B 2 , . . . , B r , where | B i | ≥ k log 2 3 for i ∈ [ q ] and | B i | < k log 2 3 for q +1 ≤ i ≤ r . For each i ∈ [ q ], since B i is a C k -free 2-connected plane graph, it follows that"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"formula","orig":"m ( B i ) ≥ | B i | -( k log 2 3 -1) 3 k log 2 3 -7 ;","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":64.22924,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":243.59905000000015,"r":368.39937128,"t":89.11028750000003},"charspan":[0,53],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/73","text":""},{"children":[],"content_layer":"furniture","label":"page_footer","orig":"9","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":38.429269999999974,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":303.47992,"r":308.46124,"t":48.39191000000005},"charspan":[0,1],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/74","text":"9"}],"version":"1.10.0"}